<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Fulton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Fulton was an American engineer and inventor, born in Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, in 1765, who is widely credited with developing the first commercially successful steamboat, the North River Steamboat, also known as Clermont. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for twenty years, working on canals and, in 1800, on a submarine for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, the Nautilus; he is also credited with some of the earliest naval torpedoes. In 1807 his steamboat carried passengers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City to Albany and back, changing river traffic and trade in America. He designed the steam warship Demologos for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> and died of tuberculosis in New York City in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-fulton.jpg" alt="Robert Fulton" width="330" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Fulton,_1806,_London,_England,_painted_by_Benjamin_West,_American,_1738-1820_Fenimore_Art_Museum,_Cooperstown,_N.Y._N0218.1961.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Robert Fulton</b>'s North River Steamboat left New York and reached Albany in 32 hours of running.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
